DUID settings for IPv6 addressing. Additionally, NetworkManager is known to automatically reset `/etc/resolv.conf` upon startup, ensuring consistent DNS configuration.\n\nDespite its robust design and broad adoption, NetworkManager may encounter operational issues, such as failing to start after system boot or being improperly installed or configured, which may lead to connectivity problems. Some reports note that it can take control of network configurations after system upgrades, potentially causing login issues or unintended changes to network settings. However, conflicting accounts regarding its operational status—such as claims that it is stopped or not properly installed—are likely attributable to transient misconfigurations, incomplete installations, or system-specific issues rather than fundamental flaws in the service itself. All consistent descriptions affirm NetworkManager’s central role in managing network interfaces, configurations, and connections, with its functionality remaining largely unchanged across varied implementations. It is important to clarify that NetworkManager is not a directory; while it interacts with configuration directories such as `/etc/NetworkManager` and `/var/lib/NetworkManager`, it is a dynamic system service and daemon, not a static file repository.<SEP>NetworkManager is a system service that manages network interfaces on Linux systems.<SEP>NetworkManager is a system service that manages network connections on Linux systems.<SEP>NetworkManager is a system service that manages network interfaces on Linux systems.<SEP>NetworkManager is a system service that manages network connections on Linux systems."}
